 { In this section we'll cover a few typical patterns of attack that you can watch out for if you have an advantage in space. These include attacking the structure, transforming the structure, steamrolling the opponent, and a passed pawn strategy in the endgame. One of the most common ways of exploiting a space advantage is by using it to target the base of the opponent's pawn chain. In the following position, can you find a sequence for Black that allows them to do exactly this? } 1... { -KEY- } c4 $1 { Grabbing space and forcing the queen exchange, since Qb3-a4 would lead to the loss of the pawn on b2. } 2. Qxb6 Nxb6 $1 { This is the point of Black's idea: they wish to drive the knight to the a4 square and target the base of White's queenside pawn chain. The problem for White is that the lack of space kills White's piece and pawn mobility, making it impossible for them to defend. By comparison, imagine that Black's pawn was on c6 instead of c4. In this case, White would have options like b2-b3 or Nd2-b3 at their disposal. } 3. Nb1 { White makes an undesirable retreat with their knight, in order to prepare to meet ...Na4 with Rd1-d2. } ( 3. b3 $2 cxb3 $19 { and the c3 pawn falls next. } ) 
Na4 $1 { Correct! White lacks a good way of defending the b2 pawn. } 4. Rd2 { White desperately tries to hang onto the critical b2 pawn, as it is the foundation of their b2/c3/d4 pawn chain. } Rc6 $19 { A rook lift, a technique associated with the space advantage, seals the fate of the b2 pawn. } 
