 { Sometimes you will not be able to target the base of your opponent's pawn chain. In such cases, you can focus on targeting your opponent's most advanced pawn(s) instead. This type of strategy hinges on making a pawn break, a topic that will be covered in more depth later in the course. } 1. { -KEY- } b5 $1 { White plays the pawn break, putting Black's queenside pawns under strain. } ( 1. Nb3 { would be a way to keep the option of targeting the base of the pawn chain, but it's not as convincing as the pawn break since for example after } Bf6 2. Na5 { Black has } Qc8 $13 { when White cannot add any more attackers to the b7 pawn, while Black can aim for a pawn break in the centre with ...e5 next. } ) 
axb5 2. axb5 { A single exchange of pawns has not solved Black's problems. White has several nasty possibilities here. One of these is b5-b6, grabbing more space and securing two very advanced outposts on a7 and c7. Another option would be to exchange pawns, when after bxc6 bxc6 Black would be left with a very weak pawn on c6. } f5 { Black tries to generate some play on the kingside, threatening ...f4. } 3. Bd3 { By pinning the f5 pawn, White puts a stop to ...f4 ideas for now. } Kh7 { Defending the bishop to renew the threat of ...f4. } 4. Qc2 $1 { Again stopping this idea, and now connecting the rooks. } cxb5 { Finally the tension is too great, so Black captures. The drawback however is that now Black's queenside structure is left fractured. } 5. Rxa8 Qxa8 6. Rb1 $1 { Preparing to grab the pawn with the rook, when the b7 pawn will be under a lot of pressure. White is already winning. } ( { But not } 6. Bxb5 $6 { since after } f4 $1 { things get a little messy, though it should be said that White is not losing a piece because he would have the move } 7. Bd3 $16 ) 
Nf6 7. Be5 Ne4 8. Nxe4 { Exchanging knights makes sense, since the change in structure will leave Black's light-squared bishop permanently passive. } fxe4 9. Bxb5 Bf5 10. g4 { White is not concerned with weakening their kingside, since Black's pieces are not coordinated well enough to take advantage of it. } Bg6 11. Bd7 Bf7 12. Qb2 { The pawn on b7 cannot be defended, since ...Rb8 would lose to Bxb8. } Rd8 13. Qxb7 Qa2 14. Bxe6 ( { Black resigned, since } 14. Bxe6 Bxe6 { leads to a quick checkmate after } 15. Qxe7 { . So far we have seen how White is sometimes able to attack the base of the pawn chain, while other times they should target the front of the pawn chain instead. These types of attacks on the structure tend to form part of a clear strategy and in some cases can span over as much as a dozen moves. Sometimes however, opportunities to change the structure in a favourable way present themselves when the side with less space tries to free themselves from the squeeze. We'll see what this looks like in practice in the next two examples. } ) 

