 { Sometimes you do not need to actively target the opponent's structure. Instead, you can squeeze them gradually. Eventually, they will feel compelled to challenge your space advantage and, in doing so, present you with a good opportunity. Let's check out what we mean in this position, reached in the online game Carlsen,M - Sanal,V (2023) . } 1. { -KEY- } Qe2 { White has a large edge, with perfectly placed pieces and a squeeze in the centre and kingside. Worried about an attack if he does nothing, Black lashes out with his next move. } f5 $6 { Contesting the space advantage, and likely hoping to provoke g5 in reply, when the locked structure would increase his chances to survive. } 2. exf6 $1 { The strongest move, losing a bit of the space advantage but permanently damaging Black's structure in exchange. } ( 2. g5 $6 g6 $13 { The closed position would make it hard for White to break through. } ) 
 ( 2. gxf5 $5 { is another tempting direction, when after } exf5 $16 { White could hope to make use of his protected passed pawn in the long run. } ) 
Bxf6 { Now Black has been saddled with a weak backward pawn on the semi open e-file. } 3. Be1 { Setting up possibilities like Be1-g3, or Qe2-e4 followed by Rc2-e2. } Kh8 4. Ba5 { A change of plans. I'm not sure what exactly Magnus saw that bothered him about playing Qe2-e4 instead, continuing in the spirit of his last move. Nevertheless, White remains in control. } g6 5. Qe4 { White finally steps the queen to the centre of the board, preparing Re2 next. } Bg7 6. Re2 Rde8 { Black's pieces are tied to the defense of the weak pawn. } 7. Rfe1 Nd7 8. Qb7 $1 { Penetrating on the queenside and asking difficult questions of Black's unstable knights, while opening up the scope of White's rooks. } Qxb7 9. Bxb7 Nb4 10. Bxb4 $1 { Capturing with the bishop is best, since White's knight on d3 blockades Black's passed pawn and protects f4. } cxb4 11. Bc6 $1 { Forcing simplifications. } Rd8 12. Bxd7 Rxd7 13. Rxe6 $18 { White is up a pawn, while conserving all of his positional advantages. Magnus converted his advantage shortly after. We see that in this game, the threat of a violent kingside attack compelled Black to lash out, but this came at the cost of damage to his structure. From here, White latched on to the newly created weaknesses, eventually reaching a winning endgame. } 
