 { Here we have another online game with Magnus having the White pieces. Desperate to activate his pieces, Black has just lashed out with the move ...b5. Find the strongest sequence for White. } 1. { -KEY- } axb6 $1 Nxb6 2. Na5 $1 { Correct! White targets the c6 outpost, while also having play against the isolated pawn on a6. Black is already losing. } Bd7 3. Nc6 { White jumps in with a fork, forcing Black to concede the bishop pair. } Bxc6 4. dxc6 Rfc8 { Going after the isolated c6 pawn, but likely missing White's powerful reply. } 5. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 { Forced, but now the c6 pawn becomes protected. } ( { The problem for Black is that if they don't capture the knight, then after a move like } 5... Qe8 { White can push the pawn with tempo against the Black rook. } 6. c7 $18 { Next White will capture on a6, winning material. } ) 
6. exd5 $18 { Black resigned here, as the situation is hopeless. The attempt to break free on the queenside allowed White to gain the bishop pair and a monster protected pawn on c6. To make matters worse, White will soon pick up the weak pawn on a6. This example and the previous one showed that oftentimes you can win by simply keeping your space advantage stable, until the opponent is provoked into lashing out. At that point, you will often have the opportunity to change the structure in a way that favors you. But what happens in the event that your opponent does not lash out and instead waits passively? In this case, your objective will be to steamroll them! Let's show what we mean by this in the next couple of examples. } 
