 { The problem with an opponent failing to challenge the space advantage is that it can lead to them being 'steamrolled', a term that refers to the crushing advance of pawns on the side where the space advantage lies. This position was reached in the game Baburin,A - Lauterbach,I (2005) . Can you spot the move that White played in order to set up a pawn avalanche over the coming moves? } 13. { -KEY- } f5 $1 { Correct! White secures a space advantage on the kingside and is ready to march the g- and h-pawns forward. } Rd8 14. Qc1 $1 { The best square for the queen, covering the c2 square in case of ...Nb4 ideas, as well as setting up a bishop-queen battery that might prove useful as part of the attack. } Nd7 { Black moves her knight away, in anticipation of g4-g5 advances. } 15. g4 f6 $6 { In trying to parry White's attack, Black softens up her own pawn structure. } 16. h4 { A third pawn joins the fray. } Nf8 17. Ng3 { Black lacks an active plan, so White applies the principle of 'do not rush' and improves his pieces. } Rd6 18. g5 $1 { A second pawn break! } Bd7 19. h5 { And now a third! It is already impossible for Black to defend her king, and she soon blunders under the pressure. } Be8 20. Bf3 fxg5 21. Bxg5 h6 $2 22. Bxh6 ( { Black resigned. It's likely that she had made a calculation error and thought that after } 22. Bxh6 Bxh6 23. Qxh6 gxh5 { the attack on White's queen on h6 allowed her to restore material, but she may have missed that here White would be able to reply } 24. Qg5 { before winning back the pawn. This example is striking in that once the move 13.f5! appeared on the board, it became almost impossible for Black to navigate the difficulties. The possibility of being steamrolled is a constant concern for the defending side. Let's take another example where GM Baburin once again had the White pieces. } ) 

