 { Taken from the game Baburin,A - Damljanovic,B (2005). White has a dominant space advantage in both the centre and kingside, but for now things are locked down. It is clear that a breakthrough is needed, but the question of whether to go for pawn advances on the queenside or to aim for the g4-g5 breakthrough on the kingside is a matter of personal taste. } 24. { -KEY- } b4 $1 { White decides to focus on the queenside, likely figuring that it is the safer option to ensure that Black cannot generate any counterplay against his king. } cxb4 25. axb4 O-O 26. O-O { By castling, White shows that he has abandoned (at least for now) any ideas revolving around the g4-g5 pawn break. } Rfc8 27. Ra3 { The a6 pawn, lacking protection, is a logical target. } Be8 28. Rfa1 Qb7 29. Qa2 { Black's position is miserable, as White triples up on the a-file with the a6 pawn as the main target. } Nb8 { A common problem when you lack space is that you end up having to curl yourself up into a ball in order to try to keep the position held together. But adopting the fetal position usually doesn't work out well at the chessboard! } 30. Kg2 { White recognises that his advantage is stable and so tries to improve even his king before initiating the queenside breakthrough. } ( 30. c5 $1 { The immediate break was even stronger, since in this instance Black's king was in the line of fire of the White queen, opening up some additional tactical possibilities in certain variations. } ) 
Kf8 31. c5 $1 { Breaking through. White is willing to go down a pawn temporarily as he will get open lines and a passed d-pawn in return. } bxc5 32. bxc5 dxc5 ( 32... Qc7 { Black cannot ignore the pawn since after } 33. c6 $18 { The protected passer is a monster that will cost Black a piece. } ) 
33. d6 $1 { One of the benefits of pawn breaks is that it can lead to the creation of a passed pawn. This topic will be covered in more depth in the 'Pawn Play' chapter. } Nec6 34. Bxc5 { Recovering the pawn, with the threat of 35.d7+. } Nd7 { Stopping the threat, but suddenly Black's queen will find herself trapped. } ( 34... Qd7 35. Nd5 $18 { is also hopeless. } ) 
35. Rb1 $1 { The queen is trapped. } Qxb1 36. Bxb1 { From here, it is a matter of technique. I've included the rest of the game with minimal commentary. } Nxc5 37. Nd5 Bf7 38. Qf2 Nd4 39. Nb6 Rcb8 40. Nxa8 Rxb1 41. Rc3 Bc4 ( 41... Nxe4 { fails to } 42. Rc8 Be8 43. d7 $18 ) 
42. Rxc4 Nxe4 43. d7 Ke7 44. Rxd4 exd4 45. Qxd4 { Black resigned. This game showcased typical themes. At first White positioned his pieces as well as he could, before making the decisive breakthrough with 31.c5! Black's pieces lacked mobility and could not cope with the newly opened lines, resulting in the Black queen being caught in a net. Playing against a large space advantage is demoralising, with the defending side's pieces all struggling for good squares. } 
