 { There's a unique benefit to having more space in the endgame. In this phase, passed pawns can interact with the space advantage, leading to game-ending sacrifices. Can you find the winning sequence for White in this position? } 1. { -KEY- } Be5 $1 Bxe5 ( 1... Bd8 { Black can try keeping the bishops, but here White can continue to dominate the opponent's pieces with } 2. Bd6 $1 { Black has no good way of stopping the threat of Ne5+, for example } Nc7 3. Kc4 { White should avoid exchanging on c7 as that leads to a draw. } Ke6 { Black tries to improve their king placement, but no matter what they do, there is no possibility to ward off White's tactical plan. } 4. Ne5 $18 { White will take on g6 next. In the game, Black tried exchanging bishops but here White's sacrificial idea is even easier to execute: } ) 
2. Nxe5+ Ke7 3. Nxg6+ hxg6 4. h7 $18 { Correct! The pawn will promote next. Whenever you have a space advantage in the endgame, it pays to check for possible piece sacrifices as a way of creating a passed pawn. This brings us to the end of the section. When you have a space advantage, most of the time the way to play for a win will involve either attacking the pawn structure, transforming it, or - in the case of a more passive defense - pushing your pawns like a steamroller! Of course, it is not always plain sailing for the side with a space advantage. If you are not careful you can get in trouble and even stand worse. We'll be exploring this kind of situation in the final section of the chapter. } 
